# {{ if .DisplayName }}{{ .DisplayName }}{{ else }}{{ .Container }}{{ end }}

The `{{ .Container }}` function of {{ .SoftwareSystem }}, in project {{ .Project }}: an AWS
Lambda function in Python (boto3 and Powertools for AWS Lambda) behind an API
Gateway HTTP API, keeping items in a DynamoDB table, with its infrastructure
as a CDK app in TypeScript. Scaffolded by theos.

## The API

Every request is signed with AWS credentials (SigV4): the API's routes use IAM
authorization, and a caller needs `execute-api:Invoke` on them.

| Route             | Body                                | Answers                                    |
| ----------------- | ----------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |
| `PUT /items/{id}` | `{ "name": "Lamp", "quantity": 3 }` | 200 and the item; 400 for a bad id or body |
| `GET /items/{id}` |                                     | 200 and the item; 404 when there is none   |

An id is 1 to 64 letters, digits, `-` or `_`. A name is 1 to 200 characters;
a quantity a whole number from 0 to 2⁵³ − 1. Every error is JSON with a `message`.
A `PUT` replaces the whole item, so repeating it is harmless; a `PUT` sent
again with the same `Idempotency-Key` header within an hour gets the first
answer back and writes nothing. The same key with another path or body is
answered 422, and a retry while the first is still running 409.

## Develop

Open the repository in its devcontainer, which has Node 24, uv and the Go
toolchain.

```sh
uv sync --locked # install the exact Python packages in uv.lock
npm ci           # install the exact CDK packages in package-lock.json
uv run pytest    # the function's tests
npm test         # the stacks' assertions, with vitest
npm run synth    # write the development CloudFormation templates into cdk.out/
{{- if .Standard }}
make check       # what CI runs: format, lint, type check, test, synth, cfn-lint
make help        # every target
{{- end }}
```

Nothing here deploys or needs an AWS account: synth needs neither Docker nor
credentials. It bundles the function with uv: the packages `uv.lock` pins, as
wheels for arm64 Linux, from uv's cache or, for a wheel the cache lacks, PyPI.

## Tiers and operations

The stacks are built for a tier, `production` or `development`, which is the
place they run, not the code: `make synth` writes both, into
`cdk.out/production/` and `cdk.out/development/`, and a deploy picks one
(`cdk deploy -c tier=production --all`; without `-c tier` it is `development`).
The production stack is `{{ .Container }}`, the development one
`{{ .Container }}-development`, so a deploy that names no tier never changes
production.
Production keeps the tables when the stack goes, protects them and the stack
from deletion, recovers them to any second of the last 35 days, backs them up
daily with AWS Backup, traces with X-Ray and keeps logs a year; development
throws its data away with the stack and keeps logs a month. Both tiers alarm
and have a dashboard.

What the app promises whatever the tier, the backups' retention and copy
region, the recovery objectives and who the alarms tell, is in
`infra/operations.ts`, which theos renders from the Container's
`spec.operations`: change the block, and the file follows it. The alert email is the
stack's `AlertEmail` parameter, which a deploy can set per environment. Each
stack outputs `EntryPoint`, the API's URL, and `AlarmTopicArn`, the topic its
alarms notify.

Deploying (`cdk deploy`, after `cdk bootstrap` of the account) is for its
owner, with their own credentials.

## Layout

- `app/handler.py` is the Lambda entry point (`app.handler.handler`): it
  creates the DynamoDB table resource and Powertools' Logger and Metrics
  once, at module scope, reads `TABLE_NAME` and `IDEMPOTENCY_TABLE_NAME`,
  and hands each request to `answer` in `app/api.py`, a `PUT` through
  Powertools' Idempotency (`app/once.py`).
- `app/api.py` routes a request, checks its input and answers with a status
  code; `app/items.py` holds the rules, free of AWS; `app/item_store.py`
  keeps items in DynamoDB behind the `ItemStore` protocol tests fake.
- `tests/` holds the function's pytest tests; none calls AWS.
- `infra/stacks.ts` defines the stacks for a tier; `infra/api_stack.ts` is
  the main one: the tables, the function with its log group and role, the
  API with its access log, the alarms (`infra/alarms.ts`) and, in
  production, the backups (`infra/backups.ts`). The stack is named after the
  package in `package.json`. `infra/operations.ts` holds what the Container
  promises.
- `infra/connectors.ts` lists the data stores the Container's Relationships
  connect it to, which theos renders from their Connectors; the stack
  connects each to the function, alarms on it and backs it up.
{{- if has .Connectors "dynamodb" }} The
  `dynamodb` DataStore's table is made by `infra/connector_dynamodb.ts`, and
  the function reads its name in `DYNAMODB_TABLE_NAME`. New data goes in that
  table: the `Items` table (`TABLE_NAME`) is the starter's example.
{{- end }}
{{- if has .Connectors "rds" }} The
  `rds` DataStore's Aurora PostgreSQL cluster is made by
  `infra/connector_rds.ts`, and the function reads `RDS_CLUSTER_ARN`,
  `RDS_SECRET_ARN` and `RDS_DATABASE`. Query it through the Data API
  with boto3's `rds-data` client,
  `execute_statement(resourceArn=..., secretArn=..., database=..., sql=...)`,
  the first three from those variables. The IAM database user is a migration
  this repository runs.
{{- end }}
{{- if .Standard }}
- `infra/main.ts` is the CDK app `cdk synth` runs (`cdk.json`), and
  `infra/standard.ts` says what each tier means, tags every stack and adds
  CDK Nag's checks. `docs/runbook.md` says how to restore the data, roll a
  deployment back, and what to do when an alarm fires.

Read `AGENTS.md` before changing anything: it is the rulebook, for people and agents.
{{- end }}
